title: Customising tag order
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<<.from-version "5.5.0">> The tags shown on a tiddler keep the order you added them in, rather than being sorted alphabetically. With only a few tags the position carries meaning: the first tag reads as the most important, and tags placed next to each other read as belonging together.

The order is controlled by [[$:/config/Tags/SortFilter]], under ''Control Panel'' > ''Settings'' > ''Tag Sorting''. It holds a [[filter run|Filter Run]] that is applied to the tiddler's tags, and offers three choices: do not sort, sort alphabetically, or supply your own filter.

[[TagOrderExample]] is tagged out of alphabetical order on purpose, so each choice is visible as you make it.

Lists that show every tag in the wiki, such as the tag manager and the sidebar ''Tags'' tab, are always alphabetical and are not affected by this setting.

! Writing your own filter

The filter receives the tiddler's tags and returns them in the order you want. These examples act on a tiddler tagged `zulu Alpha .private mike`:

|!Filter |!Result |
|`[sort[]]` |`.private, Alpha, mike, zulu` |
|`[sort[]reverse[]]` |`zulu, mike, Alpha, .private` |
|`[sort[]putlast[]]` |`Alpha, mike, zulu, .private` |
|`[!prefix[.]]` |`zulu, Alpha, mike`, hiding the tag that starts with a dot |

If the filter returns no tags at all, the unfiltered list is used instead. A filter that is empty, or that matches nothing, therefore never blanks a tag list.

! Hiding tags

A filter can drop tags as well as reorder them, which is a way to keep working tags out of a reader facing view. Give your private tags a common prefix and exclude them:

```
[!prefix[.]]
```

! Where hiding does not apply

Two places always show every tag, whatever the filter says:

* the tag list in the tiddler editor
* the tag checkboxes in [[$:/Manager]]

These are where you remove a tag, and a tag you cannot see is a tag you cannot remove. A custom filter is still honoured there when it only reorders. TiddlyWiki decides by counting: if your filter returns the same number of tags it was given it is reordering, so it is applied; if it returns a different number it is filtering, so it is ignored on those two surfaces.

The dropdown for adding a tag is not affected either. It is always alphabetical, so every existing tag can always be picked.

! The tags operator

<<.olink tags>> returns tags in the order they appear on the tiddler. Before version 5.5.0 a tag that looked like a whole number, such as a year, was returned before the others regardless of where it was written.